<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Tickets' cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because six of its
    consumers are still in Base: DashboardService, UserEmailService, ShiftManagementService
    and AccountDeletionService read a human's holdings or the order projection through
    ITicketServiceRead, AdminDatabaseDiagnosticsService does the same, and TicketSyncJob
    drives ITicketSync. A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Tickets would therefore make
    Base reference a section and cycle (design §15.5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    Tickets is the first section shipping both a .Contracts leaf and a Contracts/ folder,
    and the split is principled: the leaf carries what Base consumers need, the folder inside
    Humans.Tickets carries public surface that is ASP.NET plumbing (TicketStubViewComponent).

    That split stands; the reason previously given for it — "the leaf must stay framework-free"
    — was measured false in G5 lane 3c, 2026-08-15. Humans.Interfaces has carried
    FrameworkReference Microsoft.AspNetCore.App since ISection landed, and FrameworkReference
    flows transitively through ProjectReference, so this leaf resolves Microsoft.AspNetCore.App
    with IsTransitiveFrameworkReference=true and could name a ViewComponent today. Keeping the
    view component out is a judgement about what cross-section consumers should have to see, not
    a compiler constraint, and nothing enforces it here. See Humans.Interfaces.csproj for the
    single invariant that IS mechanical: zero Humans.* ProjectReferences on Base; external
    packages unrestricted (Peter, 2026-08-15).

    Three of the members here exist so that Tickets is the application's only door to
    ticketing: ITicketDiscountCodes and ITicketVendorMirror move Campaigns' and
    GateVendorCheckInJob's calls off the vendor port (ITicketVendorService), which has
    exactly two injection sites — Humans.Tickets and Shell's TicketVendorHealthCheck.
    The port itself is deliberately NOT here: it sits in Humans.Tickets/Contracts/ since
    G5 lane 4b-2g (nobodies-collective/Humans#866), because no Base consumer names it and
    Humans.TicketTailor references Humans.Tickets directly to implement it.
    TicketDiscountCodeRequest / TicketDiscountKind are the
    section's own boundary vocabulary, mapped to the port's DiscountCodeSpec /
    DiscountType inside the section (Finance's CreditorLedgerLine shape): the leaf must
    never name the port's types, or the 2027 vendor swap reaches the callers.

    Everything with no consumer outside the section stays internal: the whole
    TicketDashboardDtos surface (24 types), the transfer wizard and admin DTOs, the
    contact-import and onsite-roster services, the repositories and the entities.

    References Humans.Interfaces (IApplicationService, SurfaceBudget, ExternalWrite) and
    NodaTime. Not Humans.Domain: TicketAttendeeStatus and TicketPaymentStatus are named by
    the DTOs here and moved onto the leaf with them (Budget's rule), and nothing else the
    leaf names is Base vocabulary.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
